<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seongjong of Goryeo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/961">961</a>–<a href="/years/997">997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seongjong of Goryeo was king of Goryeo from 981 to 997. Following the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choe-seung-ro">Choe Seung-ro</a>, he governed on Confucian principles, created twelve provincial governorships in 983 and founded the national academy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gukjagam">Gukjagam</a>, in 992. In 993 he faced the first Khitan invasion, ended by the negotiations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seo-hui">Seo Hui</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Seongjong, whose personal name was Wang Ch'i, was born on 15 January 961, the second son of Daejong and a grandson of King Taejo, the founder of the Goryeo dynasty. He came to the throne as the sixth king of Goryeo after the death of his cousin and brother-in-law, King Gyeongjong, in 981. At first he left the provincial lords alone, and to win over the aristocrats of the former state of Silla he married a woman of the Silla royal clan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 982 he adopted the proposals of a memorial by the Confucian scholar Ch'oe Sŭng-no and began to build a Confucian-style government, completing the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwangjong-of-goryeo">King Gwangjong</a>. Following the ideal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Classic of History</a> that a ruler should know the toil of farmers, he had district officials appointed by the central government and ordered privately owned weapons collected and recast as farm tools. Learned men were sent to the twelve mok to oversee local education, so that the sons of country aristocrats could pass the civil service examinations and take posts in the capital. In September 995 the country was divided into ten provinces for the first time, the basis of the later provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>.</p><p>In late August 993 frontier intelligence warned of a Khitan invasion. Seongjong mobilised three army groups and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a> to direct operations. In October the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> general Xiao Sunning crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> with an army said to number nearly 800,000 men, in fact about 60,000. Goryeo resistance halted it at the Cheongcheon River, and Xiao demanded the old lands of Balhae, a break with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> status under the Liao emperor.</p><p>While officials at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a> urged appeasement, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seo-hui">Seo Hui</a> negotiated with Xiao, arguing that Goryeo was the heir of Goguryeo. The Khitan withdrew and ceded the land east of the Yalu when Goryeo agreed to end its alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, though it kept up contact with the Song and fortified the new northern territories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Seongjong died of disease in November 997 and was succeeded by his nephew, Mokjong. The twelve mok he established remained the administrative divisions for most of the rest of the Goryeo period, and the wars with the Khitan continued in further campaigns until 1018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seongjong of Goryeo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.32,127.23&amp;year=984" aria-label="Korea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7715 787 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Seongjong of Goryeo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8165 1057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8165 1057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.32,127.23&amp;year=984">What was here in 984 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
